O.k., here comes my improved but still rough estimate, taking some of
your correlations into account.  Let us first say that once a key has
been hit by a paw, the (up to) eight neighbours have a hitting
probability of 90%, all the remaining ones of 10%.  So hitting the <alt>
key has a probability of 0.01 and hitting then one of its four
neighbours (or itself again) has a probability of 0.9 (=90%).  To hit
the "q" key afterwards has a probability of 0.1 times for the about 100
keys that are not next to the <alt> key times the probability that out
of these 100 the "q" key is chosen, which is 0.01.  The effects of
correlations on the long sequence of characters that follow and contain
a "y" with probability 1/4 is negligible.  So we get a total probability
of 0.01*0.1*0.01*1/4*200 = 0.0005, where the last factor of 200 stems
from the 200 attempts of hitting a key in the first second I am willing
to concede to the cat.  (You may reduce that by the appropriate factor,
if you think 200 is too many.)

This is a factor of 10 lower than my original estimate, but does not
take into account that there are up to four paws on the keyboard, so it
is definitely underestimating the true probability.  If we assume that
there is essentially no difference in hitting probability between a key
that is nearest to the key first hit and a key hit by another paw, we
should assign the 90% to all keys out of those hit by the four paws.
How many are these?  Up to 36, but since we are interested in the case
where one of the keys is <alt> which has only four neighbours and taking
into account that probably more than one paw is near the borders of the
keyboard, let's reduce this to 26.  Then the probability for one out of
these 26 keys to be hit directly after the <alt> is 90% and that for
a different one is 10%, the number of other keys being 79 (we are now
dealing with not so nice fractions anyway, so we might as well take into
account that there are exactly 105 keys).  Now we know that the "q" key
is not in the four neighbours of the <alt>.  But it can be in the 21
keys hit by one of the three other paws.  If we assume this, its
probability would then be 3/4*1/21=1/28, because there is a probability
of 3/4 that one of the keys *not* under the first paw is following the
<alt> and it has a probability of 1/21 to be hit among these.  But what
is the probability for the "q" key to be under one of the three other
paws?  Since I don't know anything about the relative positions of the
paws, the best guess is 3 times average area covered by a paw/(area of
the keyboard - area covered by first paw).  This gives just 21/100.
(Note that the increased size of one of the neighbours of the <alt>, the
<space> key does not really play a role here.)  So this will happen with
probability 0.9*21/100=0.189.

With probability 0.1, one of the other 79 keys, under which the "q" is
with probability 79/100, is hit, and the "q" will be the one with
probability 1/79.  So the total probability for the "q" to be hit after
the <alt> is known to have been hit is
0.189*1/28+0.1*79/100*1/79=0.00675+0.001=0.00775.  Everything else is
unchanged.  Then I get for the total probability
0.01*0.00775*1/4*200=0.003875, decreasing my original estimate from 0.5%
to 0.39%.

Of course, one can make the model even more complex, but the effects
will tend to cancel (as they did here - the short-range correlations
reduced the probability, the long-range ones counteracted to increase
it; if you assign a higher probability to the neighbours of the <alt>
and a lower one to the keys under the other three paws, it will decrease
again).  To cut a long story short: all of the effects you mention will
have some influence on the probabilities but none of them will change
them by more than an order of magnitude and the overall effect will be
smaller than an order of magnitude.  In fact, these considerations have
convinced me that my estimate is pretty robust.  Any more detailed
modeling would increase the complexity of the calculation without
significantly changing the result, unless, of course, I have overlooked
something important (as I did originally, not realizing how much more
dangerous the combination "<alt>q" is than "#q<enter>").  You would gain
little accuracy at the cost of a tremendous additional effort.  Which
means that more detailed modeling is counterproductive.

So, if that sounds more satisfactory to you, I may say that I estimate
the probability to be in the range from 0.05% to 5%, but most likely it
will be 0.5% +- 0.2%.
